From dedbd3d355c7c493268c735327b4bc173e2cd0ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Harshit Gupta <39994376+arelyx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 09:45:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno/xenos: resolve depth through the depth path

Depth used to reach memory through the color path, which leaves the
40-pixel halves of each 80-pixel EDRAM tile swapped, so depth read by the
CPU, sampled by shaders or blitted was wrong. Now:

- the resolve copies depth (RB_COPY_CONTROL source 4, raw copy) into
  k_24_8 with endian 0: pixels in natural order, each word the hardware's
  D24S8 byte-swapped;
- the restore still writes EDRAM through the color path, in 40-pixel
  strips across the whole row, each sampling its partner strip;
- the CPU converts between that word and Mesa's Z24S8 in both directions
  (this replaces the read-only unswizzle of the previous patch).

Every 128/160/250-pixel case of gltest/gldepthonly reads back exact, before
and after a restore; piglit fbo-depth readpixels (z16, z24, z24s8),
fbo-depth-sample-compare, depth-tex-modes and glsl-fs-fragcoord-zw-ortho
pass. A restore needs a WAIT_UNTIL before rewriting the vertex buffer's
texture coordinates between strips, or a strip draws with the next one's.
XENOS_DEPTH_COLOR_PATH=1 brings back the old path.

